-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 8: Series of upsamplin=
g blocks</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s talk about the three main components of the upsampling blo=
ck:</p>
<ul>
<li>Transposed convolution</li>
<li>Normalization layer</li>
<li>Non-linear activation</li>
</ul>
<h5 id=3D"transposed-convolution">Transposed convolution</h5>
<p>Transposed convolution, also known as <em>deconvolution</em> or <em>upsa=
mpling convolution</em>, is an operation used in neural networks to increas=
e the spatial resolution of feature maps=E2=80=94essentially performing the=
 opposite of a regular convolution. It=E2=80=99s widely used in application=
s such as image generation, semantic segmentation, and super-resolution whe=
re the goal is to reconstruct higher-resolution outputs from lower-resoluti=
on inputs.</p>
<p>Unlike standard convolution, which slides a filter across the input, tra=
nsposed convolution starts by inserting zeroes between the pixels of the in=
put feature map, effectively expanding it. The expanded input is then convo=
lved with a filter, where the filter=E2=80=99s stride<sup><a id=3D"user-con=
tent-fnref-1" data-footnote-ref=3D"true" aria-describedby=3D"footnote-label=
" href=3D"https://bytebytego.com/courses/genai-system-design-interview/real=
istic-face-generation#user-content-fn-1">1</a></sup> and padding<sup><a id=
=3D"user-content-fnref-2" data-footnote-ref=3D"true" aria-describedby=3D"fo=
otnote-label" href=3D"https://bytebytego.com/courses/genai-system-design-in=
terview/realistic-face-generation#user-content-fn-2">2</a></sup> are adjust=
ed to achieve the desired output size. For example, starting with an input =
of 1x1x100, with 1024 filters of kernel size 1x1 and stride 1, we end up wi=
th a 4x4x1024 feature map. In the next step, with 512 filters of kernel siz=
e 3x3 and stride 1, we get an 8x8x512 feature map. These are the first two =
upsampling stages shown in Figure 8.</p>
